course: dictionary
lesson: 8093
title: Remission: good news that may not last
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and remission is one of them. Use the words around it, the context, to work out which meaning fits. «In January 2025 it was reported that she said she was in remission, meaning there was no sign of the cancer for now.» The words for now are a clue that remission is not always the end.
explain: In medicine, remission means a serious illness has got much better, or its signs have gone, for a while. The word does not promise the illness will never come back. Grown-ups may say «Grandad is in remission» as good news, but gently, because it is careful news.
explain: The same word can mean other things. In law, remission is time taken off a prisoner's sentence for good behaviour. In church, «the remission of sins» means forgiveness. In formal writing, remission can mean cancelling a payment, such as the remission of fees. Ask yourself what the text is about: illness, prison, church or money?
explain: Good readers try their meaning in the sentence. If «she is in remission» means the illness has eased for now, the sentence still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Remission means a time when something bad has eased or stopped showing.
fact: «In remission» often means an illness has got much better, but it may come back.
fact: «The remission of sins» is a church phrase meaning forgiveness.
fact: Remission can also mean time taken off a prisoner's sentence for good behaviour, or cancelling a payment.
fact: Clues such as illness, prison, church or money tell you which meaning is right.
exercise: «In January 2025 she said she was in remission, meaning there was no sign of the cancer for now.» What does remission mean here? | *A time when a serious illness has eased, though it may return | A new hospital building | A prison sentence that has been made longer | A church service on a Sunday
exercise: Mia reads «the remission of sins» in a church prayer. What does remission mean? | Cancelling a school trip | Time off for good behaviour | *Forgiveness | A sudden cold spell
exercise: In «there was no sign of the cancer for now», what do the words for now suggest? | That the doctor is angry | *That the situation may change later | That it is raining | That the cancer is gone for ever
test: The school cancelled Amira's trip fee. Which meaning of remission fits this use? | A serious illness getting better | *Cancelling a payment | Time taken off a sentence | Forgiveness in church
test: Which sentence uses remission in the medical sense? | *Her illness went into remission, though doctors will keep checking. | The bill was cut by remission. | The prisoner earned remission for good behaviour. | The vicar spoke of the remission of sins.
exam: Jayden reads «She is in remission» and says it means she is cured for ever. Is he right? | Yes, it means cured for ever | *No, remission can mean signs have eased for a time, and it may return | No, it is only a word for a payment | Yes, it is a prison word
